abla\log p_{t}(x\mid c) is the guidance conditional score . Here the conditioning variable is an external condition c c , such as a class label or a text prompt. Unlike p t ​ ( x ∣ x 0 ) p_{t}(x\mid x_{0}) , the density p t ​ ( x ∣ c ) p_{t}(x\mid c) is not a single-sample transition kernel. Rather, it is the conditional marginal distribution obtained by averaging over all clean data x 0 x_{0} compatible with the condition c c : p t ​ ( x ∣ c ) = ∫ p t ​ ( x ∣ x 0 ) ​ p 0 ​ ( x 0 ∣ c ) ​ d ​ x 0 . p_{t}(x\mid c)=\int p_{t}(x\mid x_{0})\,p_{0}(x_{0}\mid c)\,\,\mathrm{d}x_{0}. Accordingly, its score ∇ log ⁡ p t ​ ( x ∣ c ) 
abla\log p_{t}(x\mid c) describes how to guide generation toward the conditional data distribution associated with c c , rather than how to reverse the noising trajectory of one fixed clean sample. Thus the two conditionals have different meanings: • p t ​ ( x ∣ x 0 ) p_{t}(x\mid x_{0}) is a pathwise conditional distribution for a fixed clean sample x 0 x_{0} ; • p t ​ ( x ∣ c ) p_{t}(x\mid c) is a conditional marginal distribution obtained after averaging over clean data x 0 x_{0} under the condition c c . They should therefore be interpreted separately, even though both lead to conditional score functions.

