A Tutorial on Diffusion Theory: From Differential Equations to Diffusion Models Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2605.22586v1 [cs.LG] 21 May 2026 A Tutorial on Diffusion Theory: From Differential Equations to Diffusion Models Jiayi Fu Yuxia Wang Affiliation: INSAIT, Sofia University “St. Kliment Ohridski”, Bulgaria jiayi.fu@insait.ai yuxia.wang@insait.ai Abstract This tutorial develops diffusion models from the viewpoint of differential equations. We begin with the conditional Gaussian forward process and show that this path admits both an ordinary differential equation (ODE) representation and a stochastic differential equation (SDE) representation. Averaging the conditional process over the data distribution then yields marginalized forward ODE and SDE formulations that transport the data distribution p 0 = p data p_{0}=p_{\mathrm{data}} to a Gaussian prior p 1 = 𝒩 ⁡ ( 0 , I ) p_{1}=\mathcal{N}(0,I) . We next derive the corresponding reverse-time dynamics, namely the reverse SDE and the reverse probability-flow ODE, both of which are governed by the marginal score ∇ log ⁡ p t ​ ( x ) 
abla\log p_{t}(x) . This leads to a training objective for score estimation and shows that the standard noise-prediction objective is equivalent to score matching up to an additive constant independent of the model parameters. We then discuss sampling methods for the learned reverse dynamics, including DPM-Solver, as well as guided sampling through classifier guidance and classifier-free guidance. Finally, we compare DDPM and DDIM with the reverse SDE/ODE framework and show that they share the same training objective, while DDPM sampling corresponds to discrete reverse-SDE sampling and DDIM sampling corresponds to reverse-ODE sampling. Setup Let X 0 ∈ ℝ d X_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{d} denote a data random variable with density p 0 ​ ( x 0 ) p_{0}(x_{0}) , and let t ∈ [ 0 , 1 ] t\in[0,1] denote forward time. Throughout, we assume that α t > 0 \alpha_{t}>0 and σ t ≥ 0 \sigma_{t}\geq 0 are differentiable functions of t t , and we define f t := α ˙ t α t , g t 2 := d d ​ t ​ σ t 2 − 2 ​ α ˙ t α t ​ σ t 2 . f_{t}:= rac{\dot{\alpha}_{t}}{\alpha_{t}},\qquad g_{t}^{2}:= rac{\,\mathrm{d}}{\,\mathrm{d}t}\sigma_{t}^{2}-2 rac{\dot{\alpha}_{t}}{\alpha_{t}}\sigma_{t}^{2}. (1) The conditional Gaussian forward kernel is p t ​ ( x ∣ x 0 ) := 𝒩 ⁡ ( x , α t ​ x 0 , σ t 2 ​ I ) , p_{t}(x\mid x_{0}):=\mathcal{N}(x;\alpha_{t}x_{0},\sigma_{t}^{2}I), (2) and the corresponding marginal forward density is p t ​ ( x ) := ∫ ℝ d p t ​ ( x ∣ x 0 ) ​ p 0 ​ ( x 0 ) ​ d ​ x 0 . p_{t}(x):=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}p_{t}(x\mid x_{0})p_{0}(x_{0})\,\mathrm{d}x_{0}. (3) To describe reverse-time dynamics, we introduce the reverse-time variable τ := 1 − t , 	au:=1-t, (4) and define the reverse process by Y τ := X 1 − τ . Y_{	au}:=X_{1-	au}. (5) Its density is q τ ​ ( x ) := p 1 − τ ​ ( x ) . q_{	au}(x):=p_{1-	au}(x). (6) Unless explicitly stated otherwise, all gradients, divergences, and Laplacians are taken with respect to the state variable. We write W t W_{t} for a standard Brownian motion when an SDE is expressed